Add CRYPTO_fork_detect_bump_generation for address-space clones.

A VM snapshot restored into several clones duplicates the address space without
a fork. MADV_WIPEONFORK and pthread_atfork do not fire, so every clone keeps the
same per-thread CTR-DRBG state and emits the same RAND_bytes output.

CRYPTO_fork_detect_bump_generation advances the fork generation on demand. The
existing per-call compare in BCM_rand_bytes_with_additional_data then reseeds
each thread's DRBG from the OS before its next output. The call is safe while
other threads use BoringSSL:

- WIPEONFORK (Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD): clear the flag page under the fork
  detect lock, exactly as the kernel does after a fork. The next read on any
  thread takes the existing locked increment. The generation word becomes an
  Atomic<uint64_t>, because that increment can now run while other threads
  read it without the lock (TSAN clean; the x86-64 fast path is unchanged).
- pthread_atfork (macOS): the generation becomes an Atomic<uint64_t> and the
  bump is a compare-exchange loop that skips zero.
- OPENSSL_DOES_NOT_FORK (Windows): the constant becomes an atomic seeded with
  the same value, so the bump still works.
- No fork detection: the generation stays zero, which already means OS entropy
  on every call, so the bump is a no-op.

